-- title:
--   Noetherian normality of the integral closure of A₀[j]
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$, an element $a \in k$, a datum `data : ModularPolynomialData q` (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ in $Y$ whose specialisation at $X \mapsto j$, $Y \mapsto j_q$ vanishes), and an intermediate field $K$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$ that is finite over $\mathbb{Q}$. Write $A_0 = A \cap K$, let $F_0 =$ `fieldOver (1*q) K` be the subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ generated by the constant series from $K$ together with $j$ and $j_q$, let $R =$ `jRing A K` be the subring generated by the constant series from $A_0$ together with $j$, and let $C =$ `jIntegralClosure (1*q) A K` be the subring of elements of $F_0$ integral over $R$. The assertion is fourfold: $C$ is Noetherian; $C$ is integrally closed; every $x \in F_0$ satisfies $x s = c$ for some $c \in C$ and some nonzero $s \in R$; and, for a Laurent series $x$, one has $x \in F_0$ together with integrality of $x$ over the subring of series representable as $r(j,j_q)/s(j,j_q)$ with $r,s \in A_0[X,Y]$ and $\overline{s}(a,a^q) \neq 0$ in $k$, if and only if there are $c \in C$ and $s \in A_0[X,Y]$ with $\overline{s}(a,a^q) \neq 0$ and $x \cdot s(j,j_q) = c$.
--
--   This is the instantiation, for the tower $A_0[j] \subset A_0[j,j_q] \subset K(j,j_q)$ coming from the modular equation of level $q$, of the standard facts that the integral closure of a Noetherian normal domain in a finite separable extension is again Noetherian and normal, has the same fraction field, and that normalisation commutes with localisation at the multiplicative set of polynomials not vanishing at the chosen point $(a,a^q)$. It supports the height-one localisation (algebraic Hartogs) arguments in the analysis of the node, and is used by the node-localisation lemmas on `frobNodePair` and by the corresponding statements for the $\lambda$-line.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_jIntegralClosure_isNoetherian_and_isLocalization.lean

